 | **Priority** | `10` | Preference over normal. |
 | **Priority 80+** | `20` | Expanded priority range (e.g., age ≥ 80 years). |
 
+### Display code
+
+The code is `<letter><number>`: one uppercase letter followed by at least 3 digits (`N001` … `N999`, `N1000`).
+
+**Letter by priority** (default map `Queue::DEFAULT_PREFIX_MAP`):
+
+| Priority | Letter |
+| -------- | ------ |
+| `0` (Normal) | `N` |
+| `10` (Priority) | `P` |
+| `20` (80+) | `E` |
+
+Values not in the map fall back by range: `<= 0` → `N`, `>= 20` → `E`, anything in between → `P`.
+
+**Per-queue custom map:** `queues.priority_prefix_map` (JSON, nullable) maps priority value → letter, e.g. `{"0": "X", "10": "Y", "20": "Z"}`. A custom entry wins; otherwise the default applies. Rules, enforced on every queue save (`InvalidArgumentException`):
+
+- keys are non-negative integers in canonical form (`"foo"`, `"-1"`, `"010"` are invalid);
+- values are exactly one uppercase letter `A-Z`;
+- effective letters must be unique: custom entries plus the default letters of the uncovered levels 0/10/20 may not repeat (`{"0": "N", "10": "N"}` and `{"0": "E"}` are rejected).
+
+An empty map is stored as `null`. The map is not mass-assignable; it is changed through `UpdateQueue` (`priorityPrefixMap`: `null` leaves it unchanged, `[]` clears it, an invalid map raises `ValidationException` on `priority_prefix_map` and changes nothing). There is no UI for it yet.
+
+**Daily sequence:** the next number is computed among the queue's tickets with the same letter **and** the same `issue_date`: last valid code of that letter (insertion order) + 1, else `<letter>001`. Malformed codes are skipped, legacy `A…` codes do not belong to the `N` sequence, and gaps from deleted tickets are not reused. Each letter restarts at `001` every day. Call ordering still uses the numeric priority, not the letter.
+
 ### Ordering and Fairness
 
 1. **Within same priority:** the **oldest** ticket is called first.
